        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9d301947-22d7-5285-8a08-aa0c3faf6a65",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Roles: Deputy Führer, Hitler's deputy\n- Works: Mein Kampf\n- Events: Hess's May 1941 flight, World War II, Hess's imprisonment, conviction, and suicide\n- Topics: historical debate\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- Rudolf Hess edited Mein Kampf as Adolf Hitler's deputy.\n- In May 1941 during World War II, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch was the person who reported Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight have been debated by historians.\n- After his flight, Rudolf Hess was taken prisoner, convicted of crimes against peace, and served a life sentence until his suicide.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6e537c7c-2c00-5928-bb5c-daee3b97352e",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
